      "name": "Foundations",
      "terms": [
        {
          "slug": "tutting",
          "name": "Tutting",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "Angular, geometric popping: the arms and hands hit right-angled shapes, one position at a time.",
          "description": "Tutting was created in 1979 by Mark Benson — King Boogaloo Tut — who took the angular figures of Egyptian bas-reliefs and hit them like a popper; the name comes from King Tutankhamun. It is an element of popping that grew its own community. The whole mechanic is to pop into a position, hold it, and snap to the next, so a dancer who makes perfect boxes but drifts between them is not really tutting. The shapes are measured against a grid of fixed points on your own body, which is what makes the style learnable rather than a matter of copying.",
          "tips": [
            "Learn the hit and the right angle before any combo.",
            "Stretch the wrists and fingers before every session — their range is the ceiling on how clean your angles can be."
          ],
          "related": ["the-hit", "right-angles", "king-tut", "the-grid"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "tut-unit",
          "name": "The tut unit",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The measuring stick of tutting: the length of your forearm, from wrist to elbow.",
          "description": "Teachers who work on the grid measure it in \"tut units\": the distance from wrist to elbow, which is about the same as elbow to shoulder, and about twice the length of the hand. A hand is half a unit, and the fingers divide it further. Thinking in units is what lets you snap shapes to the grid, because every limb segment is a known length.",
          "tips": [
            "Check a shape by counting units: two forearms side by side should span exactly two."
          ],
          "related": ["the-grid", "four-square", "tut-scale"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "planes",
          "name": "Planes",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The flat pane in front of the body where most tutting lives — and the other planes you can move onto.",
          "description": "Most tutting sits on one flat pane of glass in front of the chest, facing the audience square on. Keeping a shape on its plane is what makes it read as geometry. Advanced tutters then deliberately move shapes onto other planes — horizontal, sideways, behind the body or on the floor — which is where tutting becomes three-dimensional.",
          "tips": [
            "Film yourself from the front. Anything that tilts off the plane shows up as a skewed shape."
          ],
          "related": ["3d-space", "back-tuts", "floor-tuts", "pivot-tuts"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "point-to-point",
          "name": "Point to point",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "A way of building combos: each arm has three points — fingertips, wrist, elbow — and any point can meet any point.",
          "description": "One common way to invent tutting combinations is to label three points on each arm — fingertips, wrist and elbow — and move from shape to shape by connecting a point on one arm to a point on the other. Combined with the 90-degree rule, it multiplies the shapes you can find, and tracing from one point to the next is how you travel between them.",
          "tips": [
            "Stuck in a shape? Open one elbow to a new 90° angle — it frees a new point to connect to."
          ],
          "related": ["right-angles", "tracing", "tutting-combo"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "gloving",
          "name": "Gloving",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The light-show hand-dance scene that grew out of finger tutting — finger rolls, whips, waves and tunnels.",
          "description": "Finger tutting moved out of popping into gloving, where dancers wear light-up gloves and perform close up to rave music. Gloving added its own vocabulary — finger rolls, waves, whips, tunnels — and many of today's best finger tutters come from that scene. The moves in the Hands category draw on both.",
          "related": ["finger-tutting", "finger-rolls", "whips", "tunneling"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
